            "defaults": "pf=5&lt=3000&r=value%3D255.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&g=value%3D0.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&b=value%3D0.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&wa=value%3D4.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&wf=value%3D1.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&ftb=0&scale=value%3D1.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&opacity=value%3D0.900000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&c0=255&c1=255&c2=255&c3=255&c4=255&c5=255&usedefaultcolor=1",
            "presets": {}
        },
        "cartoon": {
            "id": 41,
            "label": "Cartoon",
            "category": "style",
            "summary": "Posterises the frame into flat colour bands and draws dark lines along the edges - the 'comic book' look. Measured on out/cut10-cartoon.mp4: cc is how many colour levels survive (cc=2 leaves 119 distinct luma levels and MAE 65.3 against the untreated frame; cc=64 leaves 198 and MAE 10.6, so low cc = flatter). et is edge sensitivity: the count of strong-gradient pixels runs 1345 at et=0, 2835 at the default 58 and 8343 at et=100, i.e. higher et turns more of the picture into outline. br is outline brightness: at br=0 the outline pixels sit 49.2 luma below the frame mean (hard black lines), at the default 5 they sit 36.6 below, and at br=20 they are level with the picture (+0.9) so the lines stop reading as lines. Bounds are left unset because only the 0..100 (et) and 0..64 (cc) envelope has been exercised; values outside it are written as given.",
            "anonymous": false,
            "params": [
                { "key": "cc", "type": "float", "wrap": true, "default": 11 },
                { "key": "et", "type": "float", "wrap": true, "default": 58 },
                { "key": "br", "type": "float", "wrap": true, "default": 5 }
            ],
            "defaults": "cc=value%3D11.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&et=value%3D58.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0&br=value%3D5.000000%26userdata%3D0%26keys%3D0",
            "presets": {}
        },
        "snow": {
            "id": 84,
            "label": "Snow",
            "category": "overlay",
            "summary": "Animated falling-snow overlay drawn over the picture. Measured on out/cut11-snow.mp4 (share of pixels that read as flakes): pf is the flake budget and scales the result close to linearly - pf=5 gives 0.07%, the default 25 gives 0.30%, pf=100 gives 1.12%. scale is flake size and changes coverage just as hard - 0.03% at 0.1, 1.07% at 2. opacity is how solid the flakes are, so at 0.3 no pixel is bright enough to count as snow at all. dv adds sideways drift (0.51% coverage at dv=1 versus 0.30% default, and more frame-to-frame motion). lt is a flake's lifetime in milliseconds; it barely moves a single sampled frame (0.22% at 500, 0.26% at 8000) but governs how long a flake survives, so it is what to change when the fall looks too steady. Unlike a colour filter this is time-based: two frames of the same still source differ once snow is on.",
            "anonymous": false,
            "params": [
